{
  "stage": "verify",
  "item": "ch03.s08",
  "inputs_hash": "c3b0779d44c27369c10be1cae50cefb4acc9ff6593373710403fd562f756a12c",
  "output_hash": "8dc625cb63c8e547ce229ac9d4c908fd28e9960a823d3f36a91015dd329b13c4",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch03.s08",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch03.s08.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p01.r01",
            "text": "How does causal attention become robust enough for training and flexible enough to process more than one sequence at a time?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p01.r02",
            "text": "Dropout is a deep learning technique where you take a neural network and randomly switch neurons in different layers to zero.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1974",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1976",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1978",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1980",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1981"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p01.r03",
            "text": "A simple way to think about dropout is that it prevents a lazy-neuron or codependency problem: if one neuron relies on others to do most of the work, switching those others off forces it to participate.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1984",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1986",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1989",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1991",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@1999",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2001",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2004",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2006"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p01.r04",
            "text": "By randomly turning neurons off, dropout helps all neurons participate, prevents overfitting, and improves generalization performance, including on test data.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2011",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2013",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2015",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2019",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2021",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2023",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2027",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2029"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p01.r05",
            "text": "In transformer architectures, including GPT, dropout in the attention mechanism is usually applied either after calculating attention scores or after applying attention weights to the value vectors.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2030",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2033",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2035",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2037",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2039",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2042",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2044",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2047"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p01.r06",
            "text": "A more common choice is to apply dropout after calculating the attention weights or attention scores rather than after forming the context vector.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2057",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2059",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2061",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2062",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2065",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2069",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2071"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s08.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r01",
            "text": "For causal attention, dropout is applied by first creating a dropout mask that specifies what neurons need to be randomly turned off.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2075",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2077",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2084",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2086",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2089"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r02",
            "text": "With dropout probability 0.5, on average 50% of the attention weights in each row are turned off.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2091",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2093",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2096",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2099"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r03",
            "text": "This operates row by row by randomly zeroing out some elements with the chosen probability.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2114",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2117",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2162",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2164",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2166",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2169",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2171"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r04",
            "text": "To illustrate this in Python, PyTorch dropout with rate 0.5 is first applied to a 6 by 6 tensor of ones before it is applied to the attention weight matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2175",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2179",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2180",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2182",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2197",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2200",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2202",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2204",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2208",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2211",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2225",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2227",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2230"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r05",
            "text": "When dropout with rate 0.5 is applied, the weights that are not switched off are rescaled by a factor of two.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2241",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2243",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2245",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2247",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2250",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2251",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2253"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r06",
            "text": "Because dropout is probabilistic, an exact number of weights need not be switched off in every row, but over many rows the average matches the dropout rate.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2256",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2258",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2260",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2262",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2263",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2265",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2266",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2269",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2273",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2275",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2278",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2281",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2282",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2285",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2287",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2289"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r07",
            "text": "Applied to an attention weight matrix at rate 50%, dropout randomly sets about half of the elements to zero and scales the remaining elements up by a factor of two.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2291",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2293",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2295",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2297",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2299",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2301",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2303",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2305"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r08",
            "text": "In PyTorch dropout, outputs are scaled by a factor of 1 divided by 1 minus p.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2323",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2326",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2328",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2330"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p02.r09",
            "text": "That scaling maintains the overall balance of the attention weights so that the average influence of the attention mechanism remains consistent during training and inference.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2333",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2337",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2339",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2341",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2344",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2346"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s08.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p03.01",
            "text": "The same dropout layer can then be applied directly to the final attention weight matrix by creating a dropout class instance with input argument 0.5 and applying it to that matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2351",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2352",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2354",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2356",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2359",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2362",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2364",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2366",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2368",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2371"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p03.02",
            "text": "After dropout is applied, some attention weights are randomly set to zero and the remaining weights are scaled up.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2377",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2378",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2380",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2382",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2384",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2385",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2412",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2414",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2416",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2418",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2422",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2424",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2426"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p03.03",
            "text": "The causal attention class follows the earlier self-attention structure but adds causal masking and dropout.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2450",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2452",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2455",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2465",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2467",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2469",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2470",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2632",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2635",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2637",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2639",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2641",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2642"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p03.04",
            "text": "Its computation is: multiply the inputs by the query, key, and value weight matrices to obtain queries, keys, and values; multiply queries by keys transpose to get attention scores; replace elements above the diagonal with negative infinity; scale by the square root of the dimension; apply dropout and softmax to obtain attention weights; and multiply those attention weights by the values to get the context vector matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2472",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2475",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2477",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2479",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2482",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2485",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2486",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2489",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2490",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2494",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2496",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2497",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2499",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2502",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2504",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2506",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2521",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2523",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2524"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s08.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.01",
            "text": "The class also needs to handle multiple sentences at once as batches.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2531",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2533",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2535",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2542",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2544",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2546",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2560",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2564",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2566",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2574",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2576"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.02",
            "text": "A simple batch is formed with two inputs so that the model receives a tensor containing two sequences, each of shape 6 by 3.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2581",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2583",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2585",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2587",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2589",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2592",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2594",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2597",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2601",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2604"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.03",
            "text": "This batched input is a 3D tensor of two input texts with six tokens each, where each token has a three-dimensional embedding vector.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2606",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2608",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2610",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2625",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2628",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2631"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.04",
            "text": "With batching, the input shape becomes batch size by number of tokens by embedding dimension, unlike the earlier self-attention version without batches.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2648",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2650",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2652",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2654",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2662",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2664",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2666",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2668",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2670",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2671",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2674",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2677",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2678",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2681",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2685"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.05",
            "text": "The batched computation still multiplies the input by the key, query, and value weight matrices to obtain keys, queries, and values, and then multiplies queries by the transpose of keys.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2690",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2693",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2695",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2696",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2698",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2701",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2703",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2705"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.06",
            "text": "In the transpose operation with dimensions 1 and 2, the focus is on the token dimensions inside each batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2707",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2709",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2711",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2713",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2716",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2721",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2723",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2725",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2726",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2728"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.07",
            "text": "Using \"keys.transpose(1, 2)\" makes the matrix multiplication possible within each batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2730",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2732",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2734",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2737",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2771",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2774",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2775",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2777",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2779",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2783",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2786"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.08",
            "text": "This transpose preserves the batch rows while transposing the matrix inside each batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2787",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2789",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2793",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2795",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2798",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2804",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2807",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2810",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2812",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2815"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p04.09",
            "text": "When queries are multiplied by the transposed keys, each batch is processed separately and the resulting attention score matrices are stacked together.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2817",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2818",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2821",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2823",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2825",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2828",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2830",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2833",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2835",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2837",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2841",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2843"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s08.p05",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p05.r01",
            "text": "Once batching is in place, the masking and normalization steps are the same idea as before.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p05.r02",
            "text": "Here, the upper triangular mask has ones above the diagonal and zeros below the diagonal.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2866",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2876",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2878",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2880"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p05.r03",
            "text": "In causal attention, positions marked by ones in that upper triangular mask are replaced with negative infinity in the attention scores matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2882",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2884",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2887"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p05.r04",
            "text": "After masking, the scores are divided by the square root of the key dimension and softmax is applied to obtain the attention weight matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2890",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2892",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2894",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2896",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2899"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p05.r05",
            "text": "After softmax, the rows of the attention weight matrix sum to one with the causal mask applied.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2899",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2901",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2903"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p05.r06",
            "text": "Then dropout is applied to the attention weights, the context vector is computed as attention weights multiplied by values, and that context vector is returned.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2905",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2908",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2910",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2912",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2914",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2916",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2918",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2921",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2922",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2924",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2926"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch03.s08.p06",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p06.r01",
            "text": "In the worked example, the context length is taken as batch.shape[1], which is 6 for the given batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2932",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2935",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2937",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2940"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p06.r02",
            "text": "The causal attention class is instantiated with input dimension D_in = 3, output dimension D_out = 2, context length 6, and dropout rate 0.0.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2942",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2944",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2946",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2956",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2960",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2962",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2969",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2971",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2973",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2976",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2978",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2981",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2982",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2985"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch03.s08.p06.r03",
            "text": "Passing the stacked batch into this class produces a 3D output tensor of shape 2 × 6 × 2, because each of the two inputs yields a 6 × 2 context vector matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2987",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2989",
              "h94TQOK7NRA@2992",
